# 7. Data Warehouse Architecture

A data warehouse integrates and organizes data for reporting and analytics.

A common conceptual architecture has three layers.

## 7.1 Staging Layer

The staging layer receives incoming data.

Common responsibilities:

- Land data from source systems
- Preserve source-like records
- Record ingestion metadata
- Separate extraction from transformation
- Support troubleshooting and replay

### Transient Staging

Data is retained temporarily and may be deleted after downstream processing succeeds.

### Persistent Staging

Data is retained for longer-term auditing, recovery, replay, or historical purposes.

Neither approach is universally correct. The choice depends on cost, retention policies, source capabilities, and business requirements.

## 7.2 Core Layer

The core layer contains cleaned and integrated data.

Typical operations:

- Standardize data types
- Handle null values
- Deduplicate records
- Standardize codes and timestamps
- Join multiple sources
- Apply data quality rules
- Integrate business entities

## 7.3 Presentation Layer

The presentation layer contains business-ready datasets.

Examples:

- Fact tables
- Dimension tables
- Data marts
- Aggregated tables
- Analytical views

## 7.4 Architecture Diagram

```text
Source Systems
      |
      v
+---------------------+
|   Staging Layer     |
| Raw / Landed Data   |
+---------------------+
      |
      v
+---------------------+
|     Core Layer      |
| Cleaned and Unified |
| Data                |
+---------------------+
      |
      v
+---------------------+
| Presentation Layer  |
| Facts and Dimensions|
+---------------------+
      |
      v
Reports and Analytics
```

