# 10. Star Schema vs. Snowflake Schema

## 10.1 Star Schema

A star schema contains a central fact table connected directly to dimension tables.

```text
               DimDate
                  |
DimCustomer -- FactSales -- DimProduct
                  |
               DimStore
```

Advantages:

- Easy to understand
- Often requires fewer joins
- Well suited to BI queries
- Clear separation between facts and dimensions

Trade-offs:

- Dimension attributes may be repeated
- Dimension tables may be wider

## 10.2 Snowflake Schema

A snowflake schema normalizes some dimensions into related tables.

```text
FactSales ---- DimProduct ---- DimCategory
     |
     +-------- DimCustomer ---- DimCity ---- DimRegion
     |
     +-------- DimDate
```

Advantages:

- May reduce repeated descriptive data
- Can represent hierarchies explicitly
- Can centralize maintenance of some attributes

Trade-offs:

- More joins
- Greater query complexity
- More tables to maintain

## 10.3 Which Is Better?

Neither is universally better.

Star schemas are often preferred for simple analytical consumption. Snowflake schemas may be appropriate when normalization or hierarchy maintenance provides meaningful benefits.

Performance depends on the data platform, optimizer, storage layout, caching, and query workload.

