# 11. Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD)

Slowly Changing Dimensions define how changes to descriptive attributes are stored over time.

Example:

A product's category changes from `Clothing` to `Hair Accessories`.

Assume `ProductID = 3`.

## 11.1 SCD Type 0 — Retain Original Value

Type 0 preserves the original attribute value.

| ProductID | ProductName | Category |
|---|---|---|
| 3 | Comb | Clothing |

Even if the source value changes, the original attribute remains unchanged.

Use Type 0 when the original value must remain fixed.

## 11.2 SCD Type 1 — Overwrite

Type 1 overwrites the existing attribute without preserving the prior value in that record.

Before:

| ProductKey | ProductID | ProductName | Category |
|---:|---:|---|---|
| 1042 | 3 | Comb | Clothing |

After:

| ProductKey | ProductID | ProductName | Category |
|---:|---:|---|---|
| 1042 | 3 | Comb | Hair Accessories |

Advantages:

- Simple implementation
- Current-state reporting is straightforward

Disadvantages:

- Historical values are not preserved in the Type 1 record
- Historical facts may appear under the current dimension attribute

## 11.3 SCD Type 2 — Preserve History

Type 2 creates a new dimension row when a tracked attribute changes.

| ProductKey | ProductID | Category | EffectiveFrom | EffectiveTo | IsCurrent |
|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|
| 1042 | 3 | Clothing | 2024-01-01 | 2025-03-31 | False |
| 2088 | 3 | Hair Accessories | 2025-04-01 | NULL | True |

The old version is retained, and the new version receives a new surrogate key.

Type 2 is useful when reports must reflect the attribute value that was true at the time of a transaction.

## 11.4 SCD Type 3 — Limited History

Type 3 stores selected prior values in additional columns.

Example:

```text
CustomerID
CurrentRegion
PreviousRegion
```

It supports limited comparisons but does not preserve an unlimited sequence of changes.

## 11.5 SCD Comparison

| Type | Behavior | Historical Values |
|---|---|---|
| Type 0 | Keep original value | Original value only |
| Type 1 | Overwrite | Previous value not retained in the Type 1 record |
| Type 2 | Insert a new version | Full history across rows |
| Type 3 | Store selected previous values | Limited history |

## 11.6 Important Type 2 Considerations

- Use surrogate keys to distinguish versions.
- Define effective start and end timestamps.
- Define how the current version is identified.
- Ensure fact records reference the correct version.
- Decide how late-arriving facts are handled.
- Define a policy for retroactive corrections.

The exact effective-date convention may differ across implementations.

