# 13. Medallion Architecture

Medallion Architecture separates data into layers that progressively improve its readiness for use.

The common layers are:

- Bronze
- Silver
- Gold

## 13.1 Bronze Layer — Raw Data

Purpose: store ingested data in a source-like representation.

Typical practices:

- Preserve original records where appropriate.
- Record source and ingestion metadata.
- Support auditing and reprocessing.
- Avoid silently dropping source fields.
- Apply access controls and retention policies.

Raw data may include operational metadata, such as ingestion timestamps and pipeline run identifiers.

## 13.2 Silver Layer — Cleaned and Conformed Data

Purpose: create consistent, reliable datasets.

Typical operations:

- Data type validation
- Deduplication
- Null handling
- Schema enforcement or controlled evolution
- Standardization
- Data quality checks
- Joining multiple sources
- CDC application
- Entity resolution

## 13.3 Gold Layer — Business-Ready Data

Purpose: serve reporting and analytical use cases.

Typical outputs:

- Fact tables
- Dimension tables
- Data marts
- KPI tables
- Aggregated datasets
- Reporting views

## 13.4 Architecture Diagram

```text
Databases | APIs | Files | Streams | IoT
                    |
                    v
          +---------------------+
          | BRONZE              |
          | Raw / Landed Data   |
          +---------------------+
                    |
                    v
          +---------------------+
          | SILVER              |
          | Cleaned / Validated |
          | Conformed Data      |
          +---------------------+
                    |
                    v
          +---------------------+
          | GOLD                |
          | Facts / Dimensions  |
          | Aggregations / Marts|
          +---------------------+
                    |
                    v
       BI | Analytics | Data Science | Apps
```

## 13.5 Batch and Streaming

Medallion Architecture can support batch and streaming pipelines.

Streaming designs must additionally consider:

- Event time
- Processing time
- Late-arriving events
- Checkpointing
- Duplicate events
- State management
- Recovery behavior
- Latency requirements

## 13.6 Traditional Layers vs. Medallion Layers

A common conceptual mapping is:

| Traditional Warehouse Layer | Common Medallion Equivalent |
|---|---|
| Landing or Staging | Bronze |
| Cleaned or Integrated Core | Silver |
| Presentation or Data Marts | Gold |

This mapping is conceptual, not a universal standard.

